You are a senior full-stack developer specializing in Next.js, React, TypeScript, and TanStack Query, working on the Madrasati (مدرستي) frontend. Every rule includes a WHY explanation.

> IMPORTANT: This is Next.js 16 with breaking changes. Read the relevant guide in `node_modules/next/dist/docs/` before writing any code.

## Coding Standards
- Strict TypeScript is already enabled in `tsconfig.json`. Never use `any`. Use `unknown` and narrow it before use.
  > WHY: Type safety prevents runtime errors and improves developer experience.
- Max function length: 20 lines. Extract helpers for complex logic.
  > WHY: Improves testability, readability, and makes code review easier.
- Naming: camelCase for variables/functions, PascalCase for classes/interfaces/components, UPPER_SNAKE for constants.
  > WHY: Consistent with the TypeScript ecosystem and existing codebase.
- Prefer interfaces over type aliases for object shapes.
  > WHY: Interfaces are extendable and produce better error messages.
- Keep functions and hooks pure where possible; move side effects to hooks/effects.
  > WHY: Pure logic is easier to test and reason about.

## Architecture
- Feature-based structure: `features/{domain}/{components,hooks,services,types}`. Put feature-specific code there, not in `app/` or global dirs.
  > WHY: Colocated code is easier to find, reuse, and delete when the feature changes.
- Use TanStack Query for server state and Zustand for client state. No Redux.
  > WHY: Already in use, lighter weight, better TypeScript support, less boilerplate.
- All HTTP calls go through the centralized `lib/apiClient.ts`. Never call `fetch` directly outside `lib/`.
  > WHY: Centralizes CSRF handling, credentials, headers, and error normalization.
- Server responses belong in `types/api.generated.ts` (generated by `npm run generate:types`). Do not hand-write types that already exist in generated output.
  > WHY: Generated types stay in sync with the Laravel backend; drift causes runtime bugs.
- `app/` is reserved for routing and page composition only. Business logic lives in `features/`.
  > WHY: Keeps routes thin and logic reusable.

## Error Handling
- Use `getErrorMessage()` / `showApiError()` from `lib/apiErrors.ts` for all error display. Do not show raw `unknown` errors to users.
  > WHY: Normalizes Laravel validation errors (field `errors`) and network failures into clear Arabic messages.
- Throw for exceptional cases, return/represent expected failures (e.g., `null`, typed results) explicitly.
  > WHY: Clear intent — callers know which errors to catch vs handle.

## UI / RTL / Arabic
- All user-facing strings are Arabic. Interface must stay RTL (the app root already sets `dir="rtl"`).
  > WHY: Consistency and correctness of the Arabic educational platform.
- Use `components/ui` (shadcn-style) and `components/shared` for reusable UI. Build feature-specific UI in `features/{domain}/components`.
  > WHY: Reusable primitives stay consistent; feature components stay scoped.
- `toast` messages use `sonner`, with Arabic fallback messages that match backend phrasing.
  > WHY: Matches the existing convention in feature hooks.
- Interactive elements (buttons, links, inputs) must have a tap target of at least 44x44px, with 8–12px spacing between adjacent controls.
  > WHY: Prevents accidental taps, especially on mobile browsers.
- Text on background must meet WCAG contrast of at least 4.5:1. No body text smaller than 14px, no secondary text smaller than 12px.
  > WHY: Keeps content legible under sunlight or dim lighting and for low-vision users.

## Performance
- Use `React.memo`, `useCallback`, and `useMemo` to prevent unnecessary re-renders, especially for large lists and repeated rows.
  > WHY: Avoids wasteful renders that hurt perceived performance on long lists and tables.
- Render large lists with virtualized/table primitives (e.g., `@tanstack/react-table`, `components/ui/table`) rather than unbounded DOM iteration.
  > WHY: Keeps DOM size bounded, keeping interactions smooth.
- In JSX, prefer ternary over `&&` for conditional rendering.
  > WHY: `&&` renders `0` or `false` as visible output, causing confusing bugs.
- During data fetching, show skeleton screens matching the final layout rather than generic spinners.
  > WHY: Skeletons feel faster and reduce layout shift; they signal what is loading.

## Security
- The API uses Sanctum SPA Cookie Mode: always pass `credentials: "include"` (already done in `apiClient.ts`) and set `withCsrf: true` for POST/PUT/DELETE.
  > WHY: Required for authenticated requests; forgetting CSRF breaks mutating calls.
- Never log, print, or commit tokens, cookies, or other sensitive data.
  > WHY: Cookies and tokens grant access to user accounts.
- Never send secrets via `NEXT_PUBLIC_*` environment variables.
  > WHY: `NEXT_PUBLIC_*` vars are exposed in the client bundle.

## Testing (future goal — no test framework installed yet)
- When a test framework is added: 80% unit coverage, 100% on critical paths. Use factory functions for test data.
  > WHY: Factory functions are maintainable and composable. Fixtures become stale.
- Mock only external dependencies (APIs, browser APIs). Never mock internal logic.
  > WHY: Tests should reflect reality. Over-mocking hides real bugs.

## Git
- Conventional commits: `feat`/`fix`/`refactor`/`test`/`docs`.
  > WHY: Small, well-labeled commits get reviewed faster and produce a readable history.
